Hi.

On Fri, Jan 14, 2011 at 4:46 PM, Zhu, Lihua (Julie)
<Juli...@umassmed.edu> wrote:
> Henrik,
>
> When I do copy number analysis, should I pool the sty and snp array together
> or analyze them separately? I would think together, but just to seek expert
> opinion to make sure.

Typically CN analysis consists of two step: (i) preprocessing and (ii)
segmentation.  The CRMA preprocessing (and all other preprocessing
methods I know of) operates on each chip type independently.  Thus,
you run CRMA on each independently.  However, at the segmentation step
you need merge/interweave the data from the two chip types.  That is
taken care of by the CbsModel class.

See vignette 'Total copy number analysis using CRMA v1 (10K, 100K,
500K)' [http://aroma-project.org/vignettes/CRMAv1] - it illustrates
how to do preprocessing for each chip type.  There each chip is
contained in an R list (hence the lapply() and other list statements),
but it can be done without lists too.  The segmentation part is
described in Section 'Identification of copy-number regions (CNRs)'
and shows how the two chip types are merged.  It uses the GLAD model,
but the CBS model would look the same, e.g.

Say you have obtained two chip-effect sets 'cesNNsp' and 'cesNSty' -
one per chip type.  Then you can do non-paired CBS segmentation as:

cesNList <- list();
cesNList$nsp <- cesNNsp;
cesNList$sty <- cesNSty;
seg <- CbsModel(cesNList);

and so on.   Note, it is important that getNames(cesNNsp) and
getNames(cesNSty) match up, because that is how aroma figures out
which arrays from 'cesNNsp' and cesNSty' that should be paired up and
merged.

>
> The response posted at
> http://groups.google.com/group/aroma-affymetrix/browse_thread/thread/d38eefc
> 7fc2049f0/c6e77b2e9a00cdac?lnk=gst&q=binary+segmentation+with+un-paired+refe
> rence#c6e77b2e9a00cdac is very helpful for CNV analysis with paired
> reference or no reference.
>
> How would you proceed with unpaired reference?  The experiment contains 43
> tumors and 50 reference samples, however samples are not paired or related.

I take this as you want to use the pool of all 50 reference samples as
the global reference.   You need to split your 83 array data set into
a two sets.  Say you know the sample names of the tumors and
references.  Then you can identify their data set indices and extract
the two sets as:

idxsT <- indexOf(cesNNsp, names=namesT);
idxsR <- indexOf(cesNNsp, names=namesR);

You may have other ways to identify these.  The vignette 'Paired total
copy-number analysis'
[http://aroma-project.org/vignettes/pairedTotalCopyNumberAnalysis]
show another way.  Then do:

cesTNsp <- extract(cesNNsp, idxsT);
cesRNsp <- extract(cesNNsp, idxsR);

and repeat the above for the Sty chip type.  Plug it all into lists:

cesTList <- list();
cesTList$nsp <- cesTNsp;
cesTList$sty <- cesTSty;

cesRList <- list();
cesRList$nsp <- cesRNsp;
cesRList$sty <- cesRSty;

Calculate the robust average for the references:

cesRList <- lapply(cesRList, getAverageFile);

and finally do:

seg <- CbsModel(cesTList, reference=cesRList);

to set up the CBS model to segment CN ratios for the 43 tumors
relative to the pool of all 50 reference samples while merging data
from two chip types.

Hope this helps

Henrik

